) is wherefore, hm mah was easily dropped before Nk ken; it not being recollected that Nk ken here is connected, not with the preposition before it, but with the verb after it, and signifies hoc modo. The true reading, therefore, and the true sense I humbly conceive to stand thus:-  



         Hear, I beseech thee, and I will speak; 
         I will demand of thee, and declare thou unto me. 
         I have heard of thee by the hearing of the ear; 
         But now mine eye seeth thee. 
         WHEREFORE (hm le) am I thus become loathsome 
         And scorched up, upon dust and ashes?

